Good morning dear reader,
For years, content creation in India lived in the margins. It was something=
 done after college hours, between freelance gigs, quietly from a bedroom d=
esk. Visible, popular, but rarely taken seriously as work.
Last week=E2=80=99s Union Budget changed the tone. By formally recognising =
the creative or orange economy, and committing funding to animation, gaming=
, visual effects and digital storytelling, the government signalled that co=
ntent creation is no longer just a side hustle.
With 15,500 content-creator labs planned in schools and colleges, and new i=
nstitutional backing for creative education, the government has placed a me=
aningful bet on creators as future workers.
The excitement is understandable. Recognition alone can shift behaviour. Pa=
rents begin to listen differently. Students imagine longer runways. Early c=
reators already seeing returns expect momentum to build as policy support d=
eepens.
Yet the harder test lies ahead. India=E2=80=99s skilling problem does not s=
pare the creative economy. Strong execution, capable trainers, and industry=
-aligned coursework can turn these labs into well-equipped spaces that genu=
inely build employable skills. Industry participation and outcome-based tra=
ining will decide whether this becomes a pipeline or a placeholder.
If done right, the upside is large. Jobs emerge quickly, gig work expands, =
and India moves from low-cost production to high-quality creation.
The Budget has opened the door. What happens next will decide whether conte=
nt creation remains a side hustle or becomes a credible, long-term career.
